+## Context
+
+The package has no source yet (`src/` does not exist; `tsconfig.json` uses strict NodeNext ESM, tests run with `node --test`, no runtime dependencies). Later features will build on this one.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:** strict RFC 6901 string-representation evaluation; clear errors.
+**Non-Goals:** URI fragment form, mutation (RFC 6902), relative pointers.
+
+## Decisions
+
+- Put the logic in `src/json-pointer.ts` and re-export `resolvePointer` from `src/index.ts`.
+- Split into an internal `parsePointer(pointer): string[]` (validation and unescaping) so later features can reuse it; only `resolvePointer` is exported publicly for now.
+- Throw a dedicated `JsonPointerError extends Error`, so callers can distinguish it; exported too.
+- Object lookup uses `Object.hasOwn`, so `__proto__`/`constructor` never resolve through the prototype chain.
+- Array tokens are validated with `/^(0|[1-9][0-9]*)$/`; `-` always errors since it refers to a nonexistent element.
+- Tests are `node:test` files run directly by Node's built-in type stripping (`.ts` import specifiers, rewritten on build via `rewriteRelativeImportExtensions`). `tsc` is not installed here, so the type check could not be run.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- Tests need a TypeScript-capable runner; dev-only tooling, if needed, is kept minimal.
